    11	# Design analysis for the combo variant
    12	# a) Concrete behavior:
    13	#    The source task reads three target colors from the 4x4 top-left legend and
    14	#    removes any non-bg connected component that contains all three colors,
    15	#    leaving the answer on the original 16x16 canvas. That would collapse with
    16	#    D9 if we kept the old generator's solid rectangles, because a solid
    17	#    rectangle is already one 4-connected component. To make "rectangle as unit"
    18	#    real and visible, each object here is instead a black rectangular frame
    19	#    containing colored marks on the orange background. Internal marks may be
    20	#    disconnected from the frame and from one another. The fused rule is:
    21	#    identify each frame's full bounding box as one object, test the union of
    22	#    colors inside that box against the legend, discard whole boxes that contain
    23	#    all legend colors, and horizontally concatenate the surviving box crops.
    24	#    Example trace:
    25	#    - Box A: black frame + blue pixel + red pixel, but missing yellow -> kept.
    26	#    - Box B: black frame + blue/red/yellow cluster -> deleted as one box.
    27	#    - Under the old free-component logic, Box B would split into a frame
    28	#      component and an interior cluster; under the fused logic the whole frame
    29	#      region is the analysis unit, so the entire rectangle disappears.
    30	# b) Complexity calibration:
    31	#    Only one new grouping cue is introduced: the black rectangle outline. The
    32	#    output layout also uses a single fixed rule, left-to-right concatenation
    33	#    with a 1-cell bg gap. There is no branching between horizontal and vertical
    34	#    packing, and no hidden tie-break based on color ids.
    35	# c) Implicit assumptions from the old solver:
    36	#    - `components_mask(g != bg)` is no longer valid, because internal
    37	#      disconnected marks inside the same frame must stay grouped together.
    38	#    - The legend must be excluded before object search; otherwise its green
    39	#      border would also look like a rectangle-like non-bg structure.
    40	#    - The old same-size output invariant is gone; output size now depends on
    41	#      the kept rectangle crops.
    42	#    - A new explicit assumption is added: candidate objects are black
    43	#      rectangular frame components whose perimeter matches their bbox exactly.
    44	# d) Placement legality:
    45	#    The generator places each object by reserving its whole bbox, not only its
    46	#    visible non-bg cells. That prevents nested frames, overlap, out-of-bounds
    47	#    placements, or ambiguous touching between frames. A 1-cell border is kept
    48	#    around the whole scene so `_bg_boundary` still recovers the orange bg.
    49	# e) Visual observability:
    50	#    Humans can see the grouping cue directly: the rectangles are literal black
    51	#    containers. The important contrast with the source task is that disconnected
    52	#    marks inside one container are still judged together, and the output is a
    53	#    strip of cropped containers rather than a cleaned original canvas.
    54	# f) Visual cleanliness:
    55	#    Objects are separated by at least one bg cell in the input, and output
    56	#    crops are separated by exactly one bg column. Each crop keeps its own frame,
    57	#    so individual retained units remain easy to parse.
    58	# g) Change inferability:
    59	#    No color remapping is introduced. Colors keep their original identities;
    60	#    only the grouping rule changes from "free connected blob" to "contents of a
    61	#    visible rectangle", and the D14 part makes that grouping visible again in
